From 694a1ae3cabb75190319a7caff4485df8393c616 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 14:13:12 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] DOS-PORT: patch 0193 -- the pushed set covers every pixel
 that changed in RAM this present

Fixes the present-narrowing under-push found by the ags 0192 verifier: on the AGS Quest demo 3,100 of 3,565
presents left the screen different from the engine's frame, and the operator saw the result on the physical
screen as chains of whole copies of the walking character, leftover speech-text fragments and garbled boxes.
The engine's RAM frame was right (a screen-level series with the narrowing off is clean); only the pushed
rects were wrong. One mechanism: the pushed set must contain every region the engine repaints.

Why it was wrong. The narrowed present (ags 0124/0129) pushes the marks made since the last present. But a
frame's marks are made AFTER that frame's background restore (put_sprite_list_on_screen(), the cursor, overlays
and GUIs all come after it), so they are repainted by the NEXT frame's restore. The region a sprite stood in
last frame is therefore restored in RAM this frame and lies in no pushed rect unless the sprite has not moved.
That is every trail. Evidence: the pushed rect [389,195 9x17] moved 4 px per frame with the stale strip
immediately behind it; at frame 1200 the stale area was 407x183 px along the whole walking path against a pushed
rect of 29x57.

What this does.
- Marks made after the interval's room restore are also kept in a second box set (same merge policy, same
  cap). At the next present they are added to that present's set, tagged as source "carry" in the dirtysrc
  counters, and are not re-recorded as post-restore marks (that would carry them forever).
- A whole-surface invalidation after the restore latches the NEXT present full. invalidate_all_rects() and
  invalidate_all_camera_rects() also latch THIS present full: they mark WHOLESCREENDIRTY inside the engine's own
  dirty structure and never told the accumulator, and their callers include Display() boxes (display.cpp),
  room changes, fades, palette changes and the drawing-surface API. The experiment of latching only these two
  moved the first stale frame from 14 to 149 and cut stale pixels from 31.8M to 10.1M, which is why the two
  parts are one mechanism and not two.
- "After the restore" is tracked (the room restore is the boundary), not assumed, so a pre-restore mark is not
  carried twice and a full latch before the restore does not cost a second full push.
Counters: a dirtycarry line (presents that carried boxes, boxes carried, full latches carried).

The verifier (ags 0192) is the oracle, not this account. Cost is expected and pre-registered in PLAN.md: about
1.6x the union area for moving content, unchanged for in-place animation; a cost above it is reported and not
optimised here.

